\insrsid1259789 Motivation for the Project 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\keep\widctlpar\noline\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid3157979 \fs21\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning20\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid3157979 Most participants in the suppl
y chain today rely on forecasts of consumer demand rather than actual consumer demand to schedule and drive their individual activities. Because these activities often occur within silos, response to actual consumer demand is difficult to match and even m
o
re difficult to optimize. The resulting overall supply chain is not synchronized to consumer demand and in its current form, prevents the real-time flow of information to the supply chain partners, and adversely affects product replenishment flow to consu
mers. 
\par To insure themselves against adverse consumer replenishment situations, suppliers, manufacturers and retailers have created extensive buffers of distribution centers and warehouses. These huge safety nets are by their essence inefficient because th
ey consume enormous amounts of capital resources. Still with these investments in buffers, the consumer still experiences an inordinate amount of out-of-stock situations at the point of purchase. This is a lost revenue opportunity anytime consumers are un
able to purchase product that is in the }{\insrsid3157979 s}{\insrsid3157979 upply-chain, or worse yet not manufactured.
\par Procter & Gamble has developed a vision of how to better service their consumers, indeed all consumers, by re-architecting their supply chain from a forecast driven proce
ss into a consumer demand driven process. This will require significant advances in three distinct areas: 1) real-time information flow of the consumer demand signal throughout the supply chain partners; 2) optimization processes that return increasing va
lue to each participant in the supply chain; and 3) the development of information exchanges that facilitate the real-time flow of the demand signal and the optimization process. 
\par The si}{\insrsid3157979 ngle driving concept behind this }{\insrsid3157979 project is that consumer dem
and, not forecasts must drive the entire supply-chain. In order to evolve the supply chain from a forecast driven to a demand driven system, the following basic capability must become pervasive: }{\insrsid3157979\charrsid5262950 
the Supply Chain must have a real-time consumer demand driven signal that initiates the supply chain response.}{\insrsid3157979 
\par Consequently, Procter & Gamble and Microsoft embarked on a joint learning expedition, to explore the business and technical issues around the capture and orchestration of the consumer demand signal. The re
sult was the development of the Consumer Demand Signal Service.}{\insrsid3157979 
\par }{\insrsid5262950 This pilot project will deploy the Consumer Demand Signal Service within }{\field{\*\fldinst {\insrsid10294828  STYLEREF  "Retailer Char"  \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid10294828 Retailer}}}{
\insrsid8091327 \rquote s}{\insrsid5262950  enterprise.}{\insrsid5262950\charrsid5262950  }{\insrsid5262950 Through this engagement, }{\field{\*\fldinst {\insrsid10294828  STYLEREF  "Retailer Char"  \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt {
\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid10294828 Retailer}}}{\insrsid5262950  will gain an understanding of the business impact of deploying this solution and how to best integrate it into their existing operational framework. }{\insrsid10294828 
\par }{\insrsid8091327 
\par }{\insrsid5262950 
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \li-360\ri0\sb400\sa160\keepn\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp120\brdrcf15 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin0\lin-360\rtlgutter\itap0 \b\f36\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
